<section xml:id="exercises">
      <title></title>

      <subsection>
        <title>Isomorphism</title>

        <p>This is an important fact that we have used implicitly in class that I want you to work through explicitly as an exercise:</p>

        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>Let <m>S \subseteq V</m> be a linearly independent subset of a vector space. Then <m>S</m> is a basis for <m>\text{span}(S)</m>.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>



        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>Let <m>T: U \to V</m> be a linear transformation. Let <m>B</m> be a basis for <m>U</m>. Suppose that <m>\ker(T) = \{\vec{0}\}</m>. Show that <m>T(B) = \{T(\vec{v}): \vec{v} \in B\}</m> is a basis for <m>\text{Im}(T)</m>.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>



        <p>We call a linear transformation an <term>isomorphism</term> if it is also a bijection. That is, if it is both one-to-one and onto.</p>

        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>Let <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> be finite dimensional vector spaces of the same dimension. Then let <m>T: U \to V</m>. Show that if <m>\text{rank}(T) = \text{dim}(U)</m>, then <m>T</m> is a linear isomorphism.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>


      </subsection>

      <subsection>
        <title>Unexpected Values</title>

        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>What is the expected number of rolls of a six-sided die until you roll your first <m>6</m>?</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>



        <exercise>
          <title>Expected profit from repeated investor pitches</title>
          <introduction>
            <p>An entrepreneur repeatedly pitches a business idea to potential investors. Each pitch is successful with probability <m>1/5</m> independently of all previous pitches. The entrepreneur stops pitching as soon as the first successful pitch occurs. Each pitch costs $200 to prepare and deliver. If a pitch is successful, the entrepreneur receives $8,000 in funding. Let <m>N</m> be the random variable denoting the total number of pitches made, including the successful pitch.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Find <m>\mathbb{E}[N]</m>.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Let <m>Y</m> be the random variable denoting the entrepreneur’s net profit after the first successful pitch. Express <m>Y</m> in terms of <m>N</m>.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Compute <m>\mathbb{E}[Y]</m>.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Suppose instead that each pitch costs $1,000. Compute the entrepreneur’s expected net profit.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>More generally, suppose each pitch costs $<m>c</m>. Find the value of <m>c</m> for which the entrepreneur’s expected net profit is exactly zero.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>


        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>Subdivide a circle of radius <m>R</m> centered at <m>(0,0)</m> into cells. Suppose that the expected length of the intersection of any line through <m>(0,0)</m> with any given cell is at least <m>L</m> conditional on the line intersecting it. Show that the expected total number of cells intersected by a ray emanating from <m>(0,0)</m> is at most <m>2R/L</m>.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>

        <proof>
  <p>
    Suppose there are <m>n</m> cells and label them
    <m>1, \dots, n</m>. For each cell, let <m>X_i</m> be the
    random variable that is <m>1</m> if the line intersects cell
    <m>i</m> and <m>0</m> otherwise.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let <m>Y_i</m> be the random variable that gives the length
    of intersection with the cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let <m>X</m> be the random variable that is the total number
    of cells intersected by the line. <alert>Show that</alert>
  </p>

  <me>
    \mathbb{E}[X]
    =
    \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbb{E}[X_i].
  </me>

  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>

  <p>
    Similarly, the total length of the intersection of the line
    with the circle is the sum of its lengths intersected with
    each cell. Thus,
  </p>

  <me>
    2R = \sum_{i=1}^{n} Y_i.
  </me>

  <p>
    Furthermore, since the intersection is <m>0</m> unless it
    occurs, we can rewrite this as
  </p>

  <me>
    2R = \sum_{i=1}^{n} Y_i X_i.
  </me>

  <p>
    Note that, since <m>X_i</m> is an indicator random variable,
    <m>\mathbb{E}[X_i] = \operatorname{Pr}(X_i = 1)</m> and
    <m>
      \mathbb{E}[X_iY_i]
      =
      \operatorname{Pr}(X_i = 1)\mathbb{E}[Z_i]
    </m>,
    where <m>Z_i</m> is the random variable giving the length
    assuming an intersection occurs. Use these observations
    together with linearity of expectation to <alert>show that</alert>
  </p>

  <me>
    2R
    \geq
    \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbb{E}[X_i]L.
  </me>

  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>

  <p>
    Put this together and <alert>complete the proof</alert>.
  </p>

  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
  <p><fillin characters="70"/></p>
</proof>
      </subsection>
    </section>